Foreword of advice: I suggest you search for videos on YouTube for a better idea on what the game is like, because, as always, a textual review accounts for very little and does not give a proper impression of what this (or any game, for that matter) is like. There is a demo available, you should probably check it out if you are interested in this game. Also, for a more intricate look into skills/stats/characters, feel free to visit a community wikia at
TD;DR #1: My opinion of this game - Amazing, even if with a few pretty major problems;
TL;DR #2: Short summary of impressions (Good points, Debatable poins, Negative points):
Good points:
+ Randomly generated - alot of replayability and variety in your gameplay, each game will be different; You will get your money's worth, if you like these sort of games;
+ Many character classes (as of Gold Edition - 8 + 1 secret character) - different strengths/weaknesses which must be accounted for;
+ Simple and functional graphics - this will run on a potato;
+ (personal) Gameplay - I enjoy these types of games and this is one of the few games that really nails it, think NetHack and you will probably understand what kind of game this is, minus the item interaction hilarity that NetHack had;
+ Bloody hard as nails;
Debatable points (these are as subjective as they can be):
+/- Relatively simplistic gameplay - I like the simplicity, but there's really not alot of nuance to it. Although many advocate that your skill (decision making) is more important, I do not believe it is so, as not finding any equipment by about mid-game is NOT salvagable by your skill;
+/- Randomly generated levels can dictate how easy/hard your game is going to be;
+/- Some characters are easier to play with - Some characters start out with psionic powers (Psion(Mindgames)/Seeker(Gold Edition)). This allows them to be less dependant on luck and more dependant on skill due to having all the tools necessary (Psionic powers in this case), which allows them to be less dependant on equipment for starting levels;
Negative points:
- Each run will require alot of time to play - clearing even 10 floors can easily take about 2 hours for me (as I have a tendency of trying to clear the whole floor) and as you go deeper, each  level requires more time, due to stronger enemies, unless you are fortunate with your gear;
- This game features unlockable recipes (crafting), which you get from Consoles. Unlocking all recipes (or some potent ones) will require alot of time "grinding". This game does not require crafting to win, but some recipes are nearly essential for survival (Aggressive Antibiotics, for example. Without this, you are very often going to die on your run);
- Bloody hard as nails, damn it!
A short review of the content:
Sword of the Stars: The Pit is a roguelike game, where the levels are randomly generated (rooms/loot/enemies), but to some extent (i.e. you will see harder enemies/better loot the deeper you go). There are multiple characters which you can play as. During the game, you gain levels for your character upon gaining enough experience points. Each character starts with different strengths/weaknesses and different statistic and skill point gains.
There are 5 difficulty settings, with the most notable is 'Easy', because it is not, and also, you can only unlock first 50 messages (Messages #1-50), which do contain very good recipes, but will limit the recipe unlockability. Anything above 'Easy' will allow un unlock all messages.
At the most basic level, the combat is very simplistic, all you can really do is choose your weapon (good luck with having any!), aim at a target and shoot, hoping the dice rolls go in your favor, although Psionic Powers (Mindgames and Gold Edition only) add additional threats (now there are monsters that use psionic powers) and elements in combat (you can use psionic powers as well).
The resources are extremely scarce at the beginning (because you don't find weapon lockers too often on early levels). And the deeper you go, the more you have to try to avoid combat, since enemies get alot stronger and there is more of them. This game can be really harsh on the dice rolls and on more than one occasion, I actually didn't believe the silliness of the dice rolls I got. Also, items (armour/weapons) have durability, which can decrease on successful use, depending on your skill.
This game features crafting. Now, this is one of the biggest problems I have with the game - to create something, you require a recipe telling you what you need to create this something (you do not need to unlock the recipe to craft items, but, for the purpose of the exercise, I assume we do not use external resources to find out the recipes before we unlock them). Unlocking those recipes requires alot of time, as out of all the messages (I believe it it about 250+), less than half contain some recipe.
The process of unlocking is: finding a console (may requires repair - chance to fail), downloading a message (chance to fail) and decoding a message (you have a percentage chance to decode each word, rather than the whole message, which causes you needing to get the same message multiple times to get the recipe - although sometimes you can figure out the recipe from reading unfinished messages. After crafting something, you will be able to see the recipe, regardless of whether or not you had it decoded fully). Some recipes are damn near essential for living - examples of such are Aggressive Antibiotics (must have, unfortunately) and Hero Sotswitch (less must have, but still a must have).
The "grinding" of recipes, along with the general time it takes to play out a single run (I often clock at about 3-4 hours for about a 16 level run due to my tendency of clearing the whole floor) means that it will take a long time before you unlock all the recipes. And the best character to unlock these recipes (Engineer) is extremely poor in combat, which means you will die alot more often.
In conclusion, this game is amazing, even if it does have a few flaws (I am sure only a few of what other people may have found). It has amazing replayability, simple combat (which is a flaw to some degree, but I really like it), although somewhat alleviated by psionic powers (but not all characters can get them, realistically speaking). The variety of characters is beneficial to the gameplay, there are also alot of items/weapons/armour. Also, Kerberos (the developer) are a really great company, they released 2 DLC, which are actually expansions, because each adds alot of content (Mindgames - not only new items/enemies/whatnot, but an entirely new gameplay element - Psionic powers; Gold Edition - no new gameplay elements added, but, in my opinion, still totally worth it; a lot of content was added - new classes/items/etc).
This game will eat your time, which is both a blessing and a curse...
..and trust me - there will be alot of cursing involved. Good luck!
P.S. The Casual genre tag is an absolutely ludicrous claim, this game will kick you in the groin and laugh in your face, while savoring your tears of frustration. I guarantee you, you WILL rage at some point during the game, because this game is bloody hard as nails.

VERY good metroidvania, even though a little bit too short (your first run will probably be 6 hours without going for 100%), I would have loved a couple more areas and bosses, but what I got here is so good that I don't mind at all.
+ Controls feel VERY good, not once have I died or even took damage because of them, if you mess up it is 100% your fault;
+ 3 + 1 difficulty settings;
+ Nonlinear;
+ Lots of helpful items, everything's useful for a different scenario, but you never find yourself depending on them;
+ A fair amount of secrets and illusory walls;
+ Replay value; (2 endings, NG+, unlockable Insane mode, achievements for pacifist and no-death runs)
+ Well animated pixel graphics;
+ Fitting BGM;
+ Silent protagonist; (yes this is a plus)
+ Female protagonist; (also a big plus)
+ Protagonist can transform into a cat;
+ So many things remind me of Dark Souls and Castlevania: mainly the atmosphere, and also the music, the bosses, the NPCs, the item descriptions revealing bits of lore... and so on, but I don't want to sound like "lololol dork soulz ripoff xd"
- Not enough traps;
- Not enough ambushes;
- Not enough spikes;
- Not enough bewb slapping;

